Two a.m., and the fluorescent lights seemed unnaturally bright.

Eun-ji finished stocking the triangle kimbap and returned to the counter. The convenience store was empty of customers. Two hemispherical cameras in the ceiling corner quietly blinked an orange LED. That light had been there for a month. On the day the cameras were replaced, the manager had briefly explained: an AI-enhanced security system, he'd said. Three times better at detecting theft than the old one. Eun-ji had thought nothing of it then.

But that night, the camera moved.

More precisely, something inside the lens adjusted microscopically. A click—barely audible machine sound. It happened when Eun-ji opened the counter's left drawer, again when she retrieved receipt paper from the right shelf. At first, she thought it was equipment noise—old ceiling pipes, perhaps, or the air conditioner fan.

Yet the sound repeated. Precisely. Each time she moved.

The next night brought the same sound. And the night after. By the fourth dawn, Eun-ji stopped and looked up at the camera. The left lens pointed directly at her. That alone wasn't unusual—security cameras face people. But something was different. When the entrance door opened, the camera didn't move. When the refrigerator door opened, nothing. The lenses followed only when Eun-ji moved.

She decided to test it.

Three a.m., choosing an hour with no customers, she moved along a path utterly different from her routine. Exiting the counter's right side, circling toward refrigerators, cutting through the snack shelves, returning to the register. A route she'd never taken in ten months of shifts.

The camera followed.

The left lens caught her feet first, then the right lens adjusted its angle toward her torso. Slowly. Precisely. Not hunting—something more sinister. It studied. Eun-ji stopped mid-step. Her heart raced irregularly.

The next morning, before her shift ended, she asked the manager: Does the camera have employee tracking capability?

"The theft-prevention AI, right? Why?"

His answer was short. The question was not returned; the subject changed. Eun-ji did not press further.

Four days later, an IT media outlet published an article. Several domestic convenience store franchises were receiving AI camera systems that, beyond theft detection, embedded employee behavior-pattern learning functions. The reporters had directly confirmed with the supplier that the feature was categorized as "store operation efficiency," and no separate notification or consent process existed for employees.

Hundreds of comments appeared below the article.

Eun-ji did not read them. That night, arriving for her shift, she looked up at the camera habitually. The orange LED still blinked steadily. When she sat down and opened her screen, the left lens quietly adjusted its angle.

Click.

That sound no longer felt strange to her. That was the stranger part. Eun-ji did nothing—did not close her screen, did not speak toward the camera. She simply sat under the fluorescent lights, waiting for dawn to come slowly.

The camera continued watching her. Those things that had already learned ten months of her movements. The way she walked. The angle at which she opened drawers. The way her lower back curved when she grew tired.

Eun-ji drew her feet together beneath the counter and sat a little more upright than usual.

She did not know why. She simply wanted to.

